      "title": "Coupled Reactions & ATP",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIa-j-1",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Define coupled reactions.</li><li>Explain the role of ATP in energy coupling.</li><li>Describe ATP structure and hydrolysis.</li><li>Explain energy transfer in cells.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>ATP</strong> — adenosine triphosphate; the energy currency of the cell.</li><li><strong>Energy coupling</strong> — using exergonic reactions to drive endergonic ones.</li><li><strong>Exergonic</strong> — releases energy.</li><li><strong>Endergonic</strong> — absorbs energy.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. ATP Structure</h4><p>ATP = adenine + ribose + 3 phosphate groups. The bonds between phosphate groups store energy.</p><h4>B. ATP Hydrolysis</h4><p>ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pi + Energy. This releases ~7.3 kcal/mol.</p><h4>C. Energy Coupling</h4><p>Cells use the energy from ATP hydrolysis to drive endergonic reactions (e.g., protein synthesis, active transport).</p><h4>D. Coupled Reactions in Photosynthesis</h4><p>Light reactions (exergonic, produce ATP/NADPH) are coupled to the Calvin cycle (endergonic, uses ATP/NADPH to build glucose).</p>",
      "activity1": {
        "timeLimit": 90,
        "badgeId": "atp-master",
        "badgeName": "ATP Master",
        "badgeIcon": "⚡",
