      "title": "Light Reactions — Electron Flow",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIa-j-4",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Describe the light reactions.</li><li>Trace the path of electrons.</li><li>Explain photosystems I and II.</li><li>Describe ATP and NADPH production.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Photosystem II (PSII)</strong> — splits water; produces O₂.</li><li><strong>Photosystem I (PSI)</strong> — produces NADPH.</li><li><strong>Electron transport chain (ETC)</strong> — passes electrons between photosystems.</li><li><strong>Chemiosmosis</strong> — ATP production via H⁺ gradient.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. Path of Electrons</h4><ol><li>Light excites electrons in PSII</li><li>Water is split to replace electrons (O₂ released)</li><li>Electrons travel down ETC, pumping H⁺ into thylakoid</li><li>Electrons reach PSI and are re-excited</li><li>Electrons reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH</li><li>H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase → ATP produced</li></ol><h4>B. Products of Light Reactions</h4><ul><li><strong>ATP</strong> — energy for Calvin cycle</li><li><strong>NADPH</strong> — reducing power for Calvin cycle</li><li><strong>O₂</strong> — released as byproduct</li></ul>",
