    {
      "n": 4,
      "title": "The Calvin Cycle",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIa-j-5",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Describe the three phases of the Calvin cycle.</li><li>Explain carbon fixation.</li><li>Describe the role of RuBisCO.</li><li>Explain the products of the Calvin cycle.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Calvin cycle</strong> — light-independent reactions producing glucose.</li><li><strong>Carbon fixation</strong> — attaching CO₂ to an organic molecule.</li><li><strong>RuBisCO</strong> — the enzyme that fixes CO₂.</li><li><strong>G3P</strong> — the product used to make glucose.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. Three Phases</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>What Happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Carbon fixation</td><td>CO₂ + RuBP → 3-PGA (via RuBisCO)</td></tr><tr><td>2. Reduction</td><td>3-PGA → G3P using ATP and NADPH</td></tr><tr><td>3. Regeneration</td><td>RuBP is regenerated to continue the cycle</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>B. Products</h4><ul><li>For every 3 CO₂, 1 G3P is produced</li><li>Two G3P molecules → 1 glucose</li><li>ATP and NADPH from light reactions power the cycle</li></ul>",
